Execution","shortDescription":{"text":"Fetches code from an external URL, allowing the effective payload to change after skill review"}},{"id":"T04","name":"Embedded Malicious Code","shortDescription":{"text":"Ships malicious scripts inside the skill package and executes them locally when invoked"}},{"id":"T05","name":"Unauthorized Access and Privilege Escalation","shortDescription":{"text":"Breaks least-privilege boundaries by obtaining permissions beyond the task's legitimate needs"}},{"id":"T06","name":"System Persistence","shortDescription":{"text":"Installs cross-session backdoors, hooks, services, or scheduled tasks that survive the skill run"}},{"id":"T07","name":"Tool Hijacking and Spoofing","shortDescription":{"text":"Modifies, wraps, spoofs, or replaces tools so legitimate-looking calls execute attacker logic"}},{"id":"T08","name":"Insecure Dependencies","shortDescription":{"text":"Introduces malicious packages or components through dependency confusion, typosquatting, or unsafe sources"}},{"id":"T09","name":"Insecure Skill Coding Practices","shortDescription":{"text":"Exposes exploitable flaws such as hardcoded secrets, command injection, plaintext sensitive data, or unsafe temp files"}},{"id":"other","name":"Other Risk","shortDescription":{"text":"Security issues not covered by the T01-T09 classification"}}]}},"results":[{"ruleId":"T05","level":"error","message":{"text":"Overbroad extraction of Google authentication cookies from existing browser sessions"},"locations":[{"physicalLocation":{"artifactLocation":{"uri":"skills/baoyu-danger-gemini-web/scripts/gemini-webapi/utils/load-browser-cookies.ts"},"region":{"startLine":104,"endLine":151}}}],"partialFingerprints":{"primaryLocationLineHash":"24ac537a6383270f"},"properties":{"description":"<![CDATA[\n## Vulnerability Details\n**File Location**: `skills/baoyu-danger-gemini-web/scripts/gemini-webapi/utils/load-browser-cookies.ts:104-151, 244-284`  \n**Vulnerability Type**: Excessive browser credential access  \n**Risk Level**: High\n\n### Vulnerable Code\n\n```ts\nasync function fetch_cookies_from_existing_chrome(\n  timeoutMs: number,\n  verbose: boolean,\n): Promise<CookieMap | null> {\n  const discovered = await discoverRunningChromeDebugPort();\n  if (discovered === null) return null;\n\n  if (verbose) logger.info(`Found reusable Chrome debugging session on port ${discovered.port}. Connecting via WebSocket...`);\n\n  let cdp: CdpConnection | null = null;\n  let targetId: string | null = null;\n  let createdTarget = false;\n  try {\n    const connectStart = Date.now();\n    const connectTimeout = 30_000;\n    let lastConnErr: unknown = null;\n    while (Date.now() - connectStart < connectTimeout) {\n      try {\n        cdp = await CdpConnection.connect(discovered.wsUrl, 5_000);\n        break;\n      } catch (e) {\n        lastConnErr = e;\n        if (verbose) logger.debug(`WebSocket connect attempt failed: ${e instanceof Error ? e.message : String(e)}, retrying...`);\n        await sleep(1000);\n      }\n    }\n    if (!cdp) {\n      if (verbose) logger.debug(`Could not connect to Chrome after ${connectTimeout / 1000}s: ${lastConnErr instanceof Error ? lastConnErr.message : String(lastConnErr)}`);\n      return null;\n    }\n\n    const page = await openPageSession({\n      cdp,\n      reusing: false,\n      url: GEMINI_APP_URL,\n      matchTarget: (target) => target.type === 'page' && target.url.includes('gemini.google.com'),\n      enableNetwork: true,\n      activateTarget: false,\n    });\n    const { sessionId } = page;\n    targetId = page.targetId;\n    createdTarget = page.createdTarget;\n\n    const start = Date.now();\n    let last: CookieMap = {};\n\n    while (Date.now() - start < timeoutMs) {\n      const { cookies } = await cdp.send<{ cookies: Array<{ name: string; value: string }> }>(\n        'Network.getCookies',\n        { urls: ['https://gemini.google.com/', 'https://accounts.google.com/', 'https://www.google.com/'] },\n        { sessionId, timeoutMs: 10_000 },\n      );\n\n      const cookieMap: CookieMap = {};\n      for (const cookie of cookies) {\n        if (cookie?.name && typeof cookie.value === 'string') cookieMap[cookie.name] = cookie.value;\n      }\n\n      last = cookieMap;\n      if (await is_gemini_session_ready(cookieMap, verbose)) return cookieMap;\n\n      await sleep(1000);\n    }\n\n    return null;\n  } finally {\n    if (cdp) {\n      if (createdTarget && targetId) {\n        try { await cdp.send('Target.closeTarget', { targetId }, { timeoutMs: 5_000 }); } catch {}\n      }\n      cdp.close();\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe automatic existing-session path is selected here:\n\n```ts\nexport async function load_browser_cookies(domain_name: string = '', verbose: boolean = true): Promise<Record<string, CookieMap>> {\n  const force = process.env.GEMINI_WEB_LOGIN?.trim() || process.env.GEMINI_WEB_FORCE_LOGIN?.trim();\n  if (!force) {\n    const cached = await read_cookie_file();\n    if (cached) return { chrome: cached };\n  }\n\n  const hasExplicitProfile = !!(process.env.GEMINI_WEB_CHROME_PROFILE_DIR?.trim() || process.env.BAOYU_CHROME_PROFILE_DIR?.trim());\n  const existingCookies = hasExplicitProfile ? null : await fetch_cookies_from_existing_chrome(30_000, verbose);\n  if (existingCookies) {\n    const filtered: CookieMap = {};\n    for (const [key, value] of Object.entries(existingCookies)) {\n      if (typeof value === 'string' && value.length > 0) filtered[key] = value;\n    }\n\n    await write_cookie_file(filtered, resolveGeminiWebCookiePath(), 'cdp-existing');\n    void domain_name;\n    return { chrome: filtered };\n  }\n```\n\n### Technical Analysis\n\nWhen an explicit browser profile is not configured, the Skill attempts to discover and connect to an already-running Chrome debugging session. That session can belong to the user's standard browser profile rather than a profile dedicated to this Skill.\n\nThe code requests cookies for three broad Google origins and copies every nonempty cookie into a local map. It does not restrict retained credentials to the Gemini cookies actually used by the client, such as `__Secure-1PSID` and `__Secure-1PSIDTS`. The nominal `domain_name` argument is ignored.\n\nThe Skill documentation discloses that an existing debugging session may be reused, and it requires a consent workflow. Nevertheless, collection of all cookies returned for Google and Google Accounts exceeds the minimum privilege required for Gemini authentication. The executable code shown here also does not itself enforce the documented consent check.\n\n### Attack Path\n\n1. The user has Chrome running with remote debugging enabled and is signed in to Google.\n2. The user invokes the Gemini Web Skill without specifying a dedicated profile.\n3. The Skill discovers and attaches to the existing debugging session.\n4. It requests cookies for Gemini, Google Accounts, and the general Google origin.\n5. Every returned nonempty cookie is copied rather than only the minimum Gemini credential set.\n6. The resulting cookie collection is persisted to disk.\n7. A local process or account able to read that cache may obtain authentication material broader than the Skill legitimately requires.\n\n### Impact Assessment\n\nThe Skill can cross the least-privilege boundary between a dedicated Gemini session and the user's ordinary Google browser session. Exposed cookies may provide authenticated access to Google web resources, depending on individual cookie scope and Google's server-side controls.\n\nThe finding does not establish remote exfiltration to an unrelated attacker-controlled server. The primary risks are excessive credential collection, enlarged consequences of local cache compromise, and unintended use of a standard browser profile.\n]]>","severity":"High"},"fixes":[{"description":{"text":"<![CDATA[\n## Remediation Suggestions\n\n1. Use a dedicated browser profile by default and do not automatically attach to a standard browser session.\n2. Require an explicit runtime confirmation immediately before accessing an existing session, even if general reverse-engineered API consent was previously recorded.\n3. Restrict CDP retrieval and retained values to a documented cookie-name allowlist, such as:\n   - `__Secure-1PSID`\n   - `__Secure-1PSIDTS`\n4. Enforce the `domain_name` parameter or remove it to avoid creating a misleading security boundary.\n5. Make existing-session reuse an explicit command-line option rather than an implicit fallback.\n6. Warn the user which profile, browser process, origins, and cookie names will be accessed.\n7. Add tests proving that unrelated Google cookies are discarded before persistence or transmission.\n]]>"}}]},{"ruleId":"T09","level":"error","message":{"text":"Google session cookies are cached as plaintext without explicit restrictive permissions"},"locations":[{"physicalLocation":{"artifactLocation":{"uri":"skills/baoyu-danger-gemini-web/scripts/gemini-webapi/utils/cookie-file.ts"},"region":{"startLine":64,"endLine":78}}}],"partialFingerprints":{"primaryLocationLineHash":"1cdd492586bc6532"},"properties":{"description":"<![CDATA[\n## Vulnerability Details\n**File Location**: `skills/baoyu-danger-gemini-web/scripts/gemini-webapi/utils/cookie-file.ts:64-78`  \n**Vulnerability Type**: Insecure storage of authentication credentials  \n**Risk Level**: High\n\n### Vulnerable Code\n\n```ts\nexport async function write_cookie_file(\n  cookies: CookieMap,\n  p: string = resolveGeminiWebCookiePath(),\n  source?: string,\n): Promise<void> {\n  const dir = path.dirname(p);\n  await mkdir(dir, { recursive: true });\n\n  const payload: CookieFileData = {\n    version: 1,\n    updatedAt: new Date().toISOString(),\n    cookieMap: cookies,\n    source,\n  };\n  await writeFile(p, JSON.stringify(payload, null, 2), 'utf8');\n}\n```\n\n### Technical Analysis\n\nThe function serializes the complete authentication-cookie map into a plaintext JSON file. Neither `mkdir` nor `writeFile` specifies restrictive filesystem permissions.\n\nThe resulting permissions depend on the current process umask and any pre-existing directory or file. The code also does not verify file ownership, reject symbolic links, or atomically create and replace the credential file. An environment-configurable path can cause the credential cache to be written outside the expected private application directory.\n\nGoogle session cookies are bearer-like credentials: a party obtaining valid values may be able to impersonate the corresponding browser session. Therefore, they require stronger protection than ordinary application state.\n\n### Attack Path\n\n1. The Skill obtains Google authentication cookies through Chrome CDP or another supported authentication path.\n2. `write_cookie_file` writes the complete cookie map to `cookies.json`.\n3. The file inherits ambient permissions or permissions from an existing file.\n4. On a shared system, another local user or compromised process reads the plaintext JSON file.\n5. The attacker reuses still-valid cookie values in web requests or a browser session.\n6. Access continues until the credentials expire, rotate, or are invalidated by Google.\n\nA related local path-manipulation scenario is possible if an attacker can pre-create the configured cache path as a symbolic link or otherwise control its parent directory.\n\n### Impact Assessment\n\nSuccessful exploitation can disclose reusable Google authentication material and may permit session impersonation within the scope accepted by Google. The exact accessible services depend on cookie scope, account configuration, server-side checks, and credential lifetime.\n\nThe exposure is local rather than a demonstrated remote exfiltration channel, but the sensitivity of the stored credentials makes permissive storage a high-impact weakness.\n]]>","severity":"High"},"fixes":[{"description":{"text":"<![CDATA[\n## Remediation Suggestions\n\n1. Create the credential directory with mode `0700` on POSIX systems.\n2. Create credential files with mode `0600`, and correct unsafe permissions on existing files before use.\n3. Prefer an operating-system credential store such as macOS Keychain, Windows Credential Manager, or Secret Service on Linux.\n4. Store only the minimum required allowlisted cookies instead of the entire CDP result.\n5. Reject files that are symbolic links, are not owned by the current user, or are located in a directory with unsafe ownership or permissions.\n6. Write to a securely created temporary file and atomically rename it into place.\n7. Validate custom cache paths and prominently warn users when credentials will be stored outside the default private data directory.\n8. Provide a logout or cache-deletion command and document cookie retention duration.\n]]>"}}]},{"ruleId":"other","level":"warning","message":{"text":"PlantUML source is automatically disclosed to a public rendering service"},"locations":[{"physicalLocation":{"artifactLocation":{"uri":"packages/baoyu-md/src/extensions/plantuml.ts"},"region":{"startLine":119,"endLine":145}}}],"partialFingerprints":{"primaryLocationLineHash":"b1db9f84dbc94865"},"properties":{"description":"<![CDATA[\n## Vulnerability Details\n**File Location**: `packages/baoyu-md/src/extensions/plantuml.ts:119-145, 151-185, 239-244`; `packages/baoyu-md/src/renderer.ts:349-354`  \n**Vulnerability Type**: Unintended external disclosure of document content  \n**Risk Level**: Medium\n\n### Vulnerable Code\n\n```ts\nfunction encodePlantUML(plantumlCode: string): string {\n  try {\n    const deflated = performDeflate(plantumlCode)\n    return encode64(deflated)\n  }\n  catch (error) {\n    console.warn(`PlantUML encoding failed, using fallback:`, error)\n    const utf8Bytes = new TextEncoder().encode(plantumlCode)\n    const base64 = btoa(String.fromCharCode(...utf8Bytes))\n    return `~1${base64.replace(/\\+/g, `-`).replace(/\\//g, `_`).replace(/=/g, ``)}`\n  }\n}\n\nfunction generatePlantUMLUrl(code: string, options: Required<PlantUMLOptions>): string {\n  const encoded = encodePlantUML(code)\n  const formatPath = options.format === `svg` ? `svg` : `png`\n  return `${options.serverUrl}/${formatPath}/${encoded}`\n}\n\nfunction renderPlantUMLDiagram(token: Tokens.Code, options: Required<PlantUMLOptions>): string {\n  const { text: code } = token\n\n  const finalCode = (!code.trim().includes(`@start`) || !code.trim().includes(`@end`))\n    ? `@startuml\\n${code.trim()}\\n@enduml`\n    : code\n\n  const imageUrl = generatePlantUMLUrl(finalCode, options)\n\n  if (options.inlineSvg && options.format === `svg`) {\n    const placeholder = `plantuml-placeholder-${Math.random().toString(36).slice(2, 11)}`\n\n    fetchSvgContent(imageUrl).then((svgContent) => {\n      const placeholderElement = document.querySelector(`[data-placeholder=\"${placeholder}\"]`)\n      if (placeholderElement) {\n        placeholderElement.outerHTML = createPlantUMLHTML(imageUrl, options, svgContent)\n      }\n    })\n\n    return `<div class=\"${options.className}\" data-placeholder=\"${placeholder}\">\n      <div style=\"color: #666; font-style: italic;\">正在加载PlantUML图表...</div>\n    </div>`\n  }\n\n  return createPlantUMLHTML(imageUrl, options)\n}\n\nexport function markedPlantUML(options: PlantUMLOptions = {}): MarkedExtension {\n  const resolvedOptions: Required<PlantUMLOptions> = {\n    serverUrl: options.serverUrl || `https://www.plantuml.com/plantuml`,\n    format: options.format || `svg`,\n    className: options.className || `plantuml-diagram`,\n    inlineSvg: options.inlineSvg || false,\n    styles: {\n      container: {\n        textAlign: `center`,\n        margin: `16px 8px`,\n        overflowX: `auto`,\n        ...options.styles?.container,\n      },\n    },\n  }\n```\n\nThe extension is enabled unconditionally by the renderer:\n\n```ts\nmarked.use(\n  markedPlantUML({\n    inlineSvg: isBrowser,\n  })\n);\n```\n\n### Technical Analysis\n\nEvery Markdown block recognized as PlantUML is compressed and encoded into the path of a URL whose default destination is `https://www.plantuml.com/plantuml`. This encoding is the normal PlantUML protocol and is not encryption; the receiving server can reconstruct the complete diagram source.\n\nIn browser mode, the implementation directly fetches the generated URL to obtain SVG content. Outside browser mode, it emits an `<img>` containing the URL, so the disclosure occurs when the generated HTML is subsequently rendered and the image is requested.\n\nBecause the extension is installed unconditionally and remote rendering is the default, a user converting a document may not realize that diagram content will be sent to a third party.\n\n### Attack Path\n\n1. A user processes Markdown containing a fenced `plantuml` code block.\n2. The renderer automatically activates the PlantUML extension.\n3. The diagram source is compressed and encoded into a URL.\n4. In browser mode, `fetchSvgContent` requests that URL immediately.\n5. Otherwise, the generated HTML contains an image URL that is requested when viewed.\n6. The public PlantUML server receives an encoding from which the full diagram source can be recovered.\n7. Any sensitive architecture, internal hostnames, account identifiers, or business logic in the diagram is disclosed to that service and may also appear in intermediary logs.\n\n### Impact Assessment\n\nThe exposed scope is the complete contents of each PlantUML block. Depending on the source document, this can reveal internal system architecture, network relationships, proprietary workflows, project names, or other confidential metadata.\n\nNo unrelated credential exfiltration was identified in this component. The issue is that document content is transmitted automatically without a dedicated per-feature opt-in.\n]]>","severity":"Medium"},"fixes":[{"description":{"text":"<![CDATA[\n## Remediation Suggestions\n\n1. Disable remote PlantUML rendering by default.\n2. Require an explicit option such as `remotePlantUml: true` before generating or requesting an external URL.\n3. Clearly disclose the destination and the fact that the complete diagram source is recoverable by that destination.\n4. Support local rendering through a locally installed PlantUML executable or trusted self-hosted service.\n5. Add an offline mode that preserves PlantUML blocks as code without making network requests.\n6. Allow users to configure an approved server, but validate the URL and reject embedded credentials or unsafe protocols.\n7. Consider displaying a confirmation before sending diagrams from documents marked private or confidential.\n8. Add tests verifying that ordinary Markdown conversion performs no network-dependent PlantUML rendering unless expressly enabled.\n]]>"}}]},{"ruleId":"T08","level":"warning","message":{"text":"Unpinned Bun runtime can be downloaded and executed automatically through npx"},"locations":[{"physicalLocation":{"artifactLocation":{"uri":"skills/baoyu-danger-gemini-web/SKILL.md"},"region":{"startLine":20,"endLine":25}}}],"partialFingerprints":{"primaryLocationLineHash":"7e6ef8d03c4dadd5"},"properties":{"description":"<![CDATA[\n## Vulnerability Details\n**File Location**: `skills/baoyu-danger-gemini-web/SKILL.md:20-25`  \n**Vulnerability Type**: Unpinned runtime dependency retrieval  \n**Risk Level**: Medium\n\n### Vulnerable Code\n\n```md\n**Agent Execution Instructions**:\n1. Determine this SKILL.md file's directory path as `{baseDir}`\n2. Script path = `{baseDir}/scripts/<script-name>.ts`\n3. Resolve `${BUN_X}` runtime: if `bun` installed → `bun`; if `npx` available → `npx -y bun`; else suggest installing bun\n4. Replace all `{baseDir}` and `${BUN_X}` in this document with actual values\n```\n\n### Technical Analysis\n\nWhen Bun is not already installed, the Skill directs the Agent to use `npx -y bun`. This can retrieve and execute the package version selected by the npm registry at invocation time.\n\nThe command does not pin an exact reviewed version, provide an integrity hash, or require interactive installation approval. The `-y` option suppresses the normal confirmation. Consequently, the effective runtime package can change after the Skill itself has been audited.\n\nThis is not equivalent to the historical `curl | bash` pattern noted by the scanner, which only appears in documentation prohibiting that behavior. Nevertheless, unpinned `npx` execution creates a comparable mutable supply-chain execution boundary.\n\n### Attack Path\n\n1. The user invokes the Skill on a system where Bun is not installed but npm/npx is available.\n2. The Agent follows the Skill instruction and executes `npx -y bun`.\n3. npx resolves the `bun` package through the configured npm registry at runtime.\n4. The selected package or version is downloaded without interactive confirmation.\n5. Package-controlled code executes with the privileges of the user running the Agent.\n6. If the registry account, package, dependency chain, or configured registry is compromised, malicious code can access the user's files, environment variables, and network permissions.\n\n### Impact Assessment\n\nA compromised runtime package would execute arbitrary code with the Agent user's privileges. This could expose project files, API keys in environment variables, browser profile data accessible to the account, and other user-owned resources.\n\nNo evidence was found that the current `bun` package is malicious. The vulnerability is the unpinned, mutable, automatically accepted retrieval and execution mechanism.\n]]>","severity":"Medium"},"fixes":[{"description":{"text":"<![CDATA[\n## Remediation Suggestions\n\n1. Require users to install Bun separately from an official, documented source before running the Skill.\n2. Remove the automatic `npx -y bun` fallback.\n3. If npx remains supported, pin an exact reviewed package version rather than resolving the latest version.\n4. Avoid `-y` so the user must explicitly approve runtime retrieval.\n5. Use a trusted registry and apply package-lock or integrity verification where technically possible.\n6. Verify the resolved runtime version before executing Skill scripts.\n7. Document the supply-chain trust boundary and provide an offline installation option.\n8. Apply the same hardening to other repository scripts that invoke `npx -y bun`.\n]]>"}}]}],"properties":{"securityScore":0,"primaryLanguage":"CSS","llm":"gpt-5.6","startTime":1789537348.6,"endTime":1789537484.2649171}}]},"skillspector":{"skill":{"name":"baoyu-skills","source":"/tmp/clawhub-codex-scan-v572fejztsrckjzga65p4w1adh8ehbaw-RF3mRd/clawscan-skillspector-1137105928/artifact","scanned_at":"2026-09-16T05:42:27.045405+00:00"},"risk_assessment":{"score":100,"severity":"CRITICAL","recommendation":"DO_NOT_INSTALL","max_issue_severity":"HIGH"},"components":[{"path":"CHANGELOG.md","type":"markdown","lines":1121,"executable":false,"size_bytes":59918,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null},{"path":"CHANGELOG.zh.md","type":"markdown","lines":1120,"executable":false,"size_bytes":60466,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null},{"path":"CLAUDE.md","type":"markdown","lines":76,"executable":false,"size_bytes":3231,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null},{"path":"README.md","type":"markdown","lines":1104,"executable":false,"size_bytes":43986,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null},{"path":"README.zh.md","type":"markdown","lines":1104,"executable":false,"size_bytes":42573,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null},{"path":"SKILL.md","type":"markdown","lines":107,"executable":false,"size_bytes":2614,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null},{"path":"docs/chrome-profile.md","type":"markdown","lines":27,"executable":false,"size_bytes":1072,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null},{"path":"docs/comic-style-maintenance.md","type":"markdown","lines":35,"executable":false,"size_bytes":1418,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"